[Music]  we are in a co-working space called  Network Hub this over here is the  reception  [Music]  the kitchen which is also a coffee shop  the hot desks which you can rent out and  over here  we have our beautiful meeting rooms it  will be pretty much impossible for us to  have access to all of these amenities as  a small business so co-working is a  really good option for us all right so  now I'll take you over to the other side  that's where our desks are  so this is us we have these two walls  over here and in the last couple of  weeks we've been building out the space  into a space that works for us it's  still a work in progress but it did  really get us to start thinking about  how we want to work and how we want to  grow as a business so in this video  obviously I'm going to show you the  space but I also want to share a little  bit about what we're trying to build and  what this business is  foreign  guys are probably a little bit surprised  right now because I haven't really  talked about this before I left my job  last month  and it was a really tough decision  um it took me about five months to  actually come to this conclusion and it  was a lot of going back and forth back  and forth and back and forth  agonizing but over the last couple of  years I realized that yes I do love  doing architecture but I also love  making videos and I also love doing  research and I love talking about  architecture and I started to feel  pretty Limited in the things that I  could do as an employee at the firm and  deep down I knew I wanted to start my  own business where I could do all of  these things and I also wanted to expand  my YouTube channel just so we're clear  without YouTube none of this would be  possible so I owe everything to you guys  and so there's the YouTube side of the  business which is the majority of the  income right now but the YouTube has  been an incredible funnel for clients  and one out of 30 of them would actually  be legit exciting projects so the  biggest thing that was holding me back  it wasn't necessarily Financial but it  had more to do with my identity I  realized that so much of my a sense of  self-worth was coming from this idea of  being an architect who worked at this  fancy firm working on these cool  projects and I'm also very conscious of  the fact that a part of the success of  this channel is because people associate  me with that  and so if I'm not doing that then what  am I take this  so when we first moved in here our space  was very much like a blank slate like we  had the space over here these two desks  and this area over here and because we  knew that we were going to be spending a  lot of time here we really wanted to  make the space work for us it helped a  lot that the owner was super flexible  with what we could do here but  essentially we wanted the space to feel  like ours while sharing it with other  people so we have three desks one for me  one for our assistant to add one for  Wrath  Raph is an experienced filmmaker and  he's going to be handling the video  production side of things RAF started  working on this full-time at the  beginning of 2022 and since then you've  probably seen the production quality go  up quite a bit one of our main  priorities was having a really large  work surface for each of us we use it  for filming and product shots and for  making models and I also want to be able  to lay out arcd drawings and scroll out  when I'm doing research and for some  reason I'm always finding that I'm  needing more space on my desk and  initially we thought about getting five  foot desks with a one foot Shelf at the  end for each of us and a larger desk for  the laser cutter but after laying out  all of the functions we still wanted a  larger surface so we decided to go with  these six foot Mega desks from ergon  office we were trying to work with the  existing color scheme in the space so  over here we got the sway desks with  this beautiful cherry wood locally  sourced and crafted in Montreal and over  here just because we knew that we were  going to be expanding so much spatially  we wanted the space to be a little bit  more muted using color  and so we ended up getting the six foot  shift desk which are super smooth in  deep black and we also got these black  pegboards we spray painted everything  black so that it kind of camouflages  into this charcoal wall these desks look  amazing in our space they're super quiet  they're heat fingerprint and scratch  resistant and they were super easy to  install it actually took us less than  half an hour but the problem with having  such a large desk surface is that we  didn't have any space for shelves  without encroaching into other people's  areas which we kind of already were  so there's a little bit of a hierarchy  to these books so everything that's on  the top shelf are the most important  books to me right now for example this  book I was referencing when I was making  the mega cities video I've been looking  at Thomas work for the house that I'm  designing in California and when I slide  it in underneath my desk then I can have  this top shelf constantly with an Arm's  Reach which is super handy because I'm  referencing these books almost on a  daily basis and I also really like how  it creates like a little enclosure for  my area I put the shelves on casters so  they can move around you can connect  them and you can arrange them in  different configurations  this is the icket show from Ikea they  come in two depths 9 inch and 12 inch  and to sit under the desk I got the nine  inch ones and stacked them in this all  configuration just so that I can slide  it underneath without taking up too much  leg room these nine inch shelves can get  a little bit limiting  especially for like monographs like this  you can see how it like sticks out a  little bit but ninety percent of my  books fit in the shelf so it was a good  compromise  they didn't have any that match the  color of the desktop so we ended up  painting the top shelves to make it look  like a part of the desktop and because  these shelves are supposed to be wall  hung and had a really ugly back we cut  spray painted and glued some cardstock  to the back and the bottom shelves I  kept it the original just so that it  gets a pop of color so when you stack  them in two the top of the Shelf comes  right up to the top of my desktop  which I have on my preset one  and if I want to slide it under I have  it on presets too  and if I want to stand I have it on  preset three  I try to stand as much as possible and  sometimes I like to sit  like  this  and that's preset four these ship desks  go from 24.5 inches to 50.2 so it's got  a really good range we align these  pegboards to sit just above rafts  standing presets so these controllers  were originally screwed onto this side  of the desk but because I wanted it to  be a little bit more flexible I just put  some Velcro on it so that I can put it  here or I can even make it disappear for  small items if they're personal items we  keep them in this drawer and for pretty  much everything else we're planning on  keeping on these pegboards because I  don't ever want to have another  conversation like this hey do you know  where the paper clips are oh my God  where did I put the exacto knife where  the [ __ ] is my tape measure we have this  electrical switch right here so after a  couple of options we landed on this  layout we've only installed it a couple  of weeks ago so there's not a lot on it  but compared to before I feel like it  adds a texture and creates a grid that  makes it feel more intentional  [Music]  when we start a video we actually spend  about two to three hours just  brainstorming for ideas and we actually  started doing this fairly recently we  started it with the Sci-Fi video and  then with the Burning Man video and then  with the mega cities video so when I  started the YouTube channel I was doing  the whole process by myself like the  ideating the scripting the research the  editing in the beginning that was fine  because I was just doing the talking in  front of the camera videos but as soon  as I wanted to improve the production  quality it became very unsustainable and  so when RAF came on board it took a huge  load off my shoulders but that actually  led to a bit of a separation in the  process for example I would finish a  script and Raph would start editing it  but we would find that a lot of the  things in the research didn't really  work visually and so we would have to  scrap the research and start over and so  it became very inefficient and so a  couple of months ago RAF had this really  great idea to talk about architecture  and Sci-Fi movies so for that we decided  to switch up the process and start  collaborating right from the start so  you have five ideas Newport City from  Ghost in the shop no Neo Tokyo tree no  night City oh  Blade Runner 2049 no  seed meat the neo-futurist concept  artist  [Music]  here is our filming station and our  model making station  this is our M1 laser cutter from X tool  and I have used many laser cutters in my  life and this one is by far the simplest  and the most intuitive let me just try  putting in a model right here  we also plan on using this like a hot  desk so if we have an architect or an  editor coming to help us out they can  sit here which is perfect because the  suede desk is super customizable you can  have up to four users and each person  can have their own settings with  pre-loaded height and once we have a  full-time local person working with us  [Applause]  this beautiful solid wood desk will be  yours  you will also get this luxury chair  which is the leather version of the  YouTube chair from urban office which  comes in all types of finishes and  colors they're super comfortable and  they can adjust to any of your ergonomic  needs so we did originally think about  placing the laser cutter on one of the  desks because we do have a fairly large  work surface because these urban office  desks are really sturdy this shift desk  can hold up to 300 pounds and the sway  desk can hold up to 360 pounds so this  laser cutter which is only 20 pounds  would have been no problem but it just  felt like a massive waste of desk space  we decided to put these on the Epic  units these are the deeper 12 inch units  attached back to back in the most  barbaric way with velcro we can store  materials underneath and on the back  side we can store leftover materials or  things that we rarely use  when you're working at a firm you will  start to get to a point in your career  where your growth starts to slow down  so  in the first few years of your time at  the firm your growth is super fast  but as you keep going it starts to slow  down but that's also when your  contribution to the firm starts to  increase  and you become more valuable to the firm  you'll probably start to make a little  bit more money because you can actually  spend your time executing the tasks to  make the machine run at this point in  terms of growth you're actually giving  more than you're gaining so when I  realized I had a really unique Niche  mixing architecture and film I saw  really good business opportunity and  because I had a very specific goal in  mind  I kind of realized that there weren't  really any way for me to gain those  skills working at The Firm I feel like  there's this mentality in architecture  that you have to work under someone that  you have to intern under someone for  like years and years and years to become  a good architect but I have come to  believe that once you have the  fundamental skills it's a matter of  gathering knowledge and there's a much  faster way for you to gather knowledge  than spending eight hours a day at the  office Magic you're trying to absorb  that information and you can do that  either through focused learning you know  taking classes or reading or reaching  out to your network and this isn't just  for architecture this is with other  disciplines as well so now I'm here  or maybe here  with the YouTube the business the  architecture and it's painful and it's  daunting but I wake up every single  morning excited to go to work  and I think for the first time in my  life I'm a hundred percent aligned in  what I want to do and what I'm doing so  I think this business is  multi-disciplinary  part YouTube part video production part  architecture part design and part  communication there's not a lot of  models to go off of but I do know that  they all have a very symbiotic  relationship I love that word on the  YouTube side we really want to increase  the production quality of every single  one of our videos and we also want to  experiment with documentary style videos  in the architecture side I'm probably  not going to work on massive projects as  of now but I'm still really excited to  start doing projects under my own name  and it's not just that in the last  couple of years I've had the chance to  work with a lot of companies that are  developing really cutting-edge  technology in architecture that I  believe is going to move the industry  forward but I've never really had the  chance to use it on a project because  most architecture practices don't really  adopt new tools because it's a huge  learning curve and it's a huge expense  even though they can improve performance  and the process so I'm super excited to  start implementing some of these tools  on my project we're actually looking for  a local editor and an architect so if  you're interested I'll leave the link in  the description if you have any  questions around any of this let me know  in the comments I know that starting  your own business can be really daunting  for example I had no idea how much all  of this background stuff was going to  cost like getting your liability  insurance and your certificate of  practice and so I will try to be as  transparent with that stuff as possible  if you want to stay up to date with what  I'm doing I have a newsletter where I  share things that I sometimes prefer not  to share in a YouTube video so I'll  leave the link to that and I also have a  Discord server where you can ask  questions to each other I'll leave the  link to that in the description as well  and with that I'll see you guys in the  next video  [Music]  foreign  [Music]

